      "proposed_laureates": [
        "A. Peter Young",
        "Bertrand I. Halperin",
        "David R. Nelson"
      ],
      "assessment": {
        "nobel_worthiness": "A rigorous theory of two-dimensional melting revealed the hexatic phase and showed how topological defects govern a universal transition. It is a deep and broadly influential contribution to statistical physics.",
        "attribution": "Halperin and Nelson developed the central theory, with Young independently contributing to the framework; the three-name attribution is well motivated.",
        "maturity": "The predictions have been tested across many two-dimensional systems, and the framework has had decades to mature.",
        "uncertainties": [
          "The theory's scope is strongest for systems with suitable interactions and defect energetics; the extent of universal behavior varies across realizations."
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "rank": 2,
      "ballot_id": "B011",
      "proposed_laureates": [
        "Antoine Georges",
        "Dieter Vollhardt",
        "Gabriel Kotliar"
      ],
      "assessment": {
        "nobel_worthiness": "Dynamical mean-field theory made strongly correlated lattice systems tractable through a powerful self-consistent mapping to an impurity problem. It has reshaped the study of correlated electrons and provides a clean conceptual and computational framework.",
        "attribution": "Georges, Kotliar, and Vollhardt were central to establishing and developing the method; the attribution is credible, though the field's broader development involved other contributors.",
        "maturity": "The method is well established, extensively benchmarked, and remains productive across materials and model systems.",
        "uncertainties": [
          "The most consequential results often require extensions beyond single-site DMFT, complicating the boundary between the original contribution and later developments."
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "rank": 3,
      "ballot_id": "B037",
      "proposed_laureates": [
        "Marc Mézard",
        "Riccardo Zecchina"
      ],
      "assessment": {
        "nobel_worthiness": "The cavity method exposed glassy structure and phase transitions in random constraint-satisfaction problems, linking statistical physics to the limits and algorithms of computation. This is an elegant, consequential transfer of physical ideas.",
        "attribution": "Mézard and Zecchina are strongly associated with the influential application to random satisfiability, while the cavity method itself has older roots in spin glasses.",
        "maturity": "The theory has been developed and tested across many random models, with lasting influence in statistical physics and computer science.",
        "uncertainties": [
          "The long-term reach of the specific algorithmic insights beyond structured random problems is less settled than the conceptual influence."
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "rank": 4,
      "ballot_id": "B015",
      "proposed_laureates": [
        "Michael Berry",
        "Yakir Aharonov"
      ],
      "assessment": {
        "nobel_worthiness": "The Aharonov-Bohm effect and Berry phase established that quantum phases encode physical information beyond local forces, with profound consequences across quantum mechanics and condensed matter.",
        "attribution": "Aharonov's effect and Berry's general geometric phase are distinct, complementary contributions. The proposed names fit the stated scope, though the broader history of geometric phases involves additional work.",
        "maturity": "Both contributions are foundational and thoroughly confirmed across diverse experiments and theoretical applications.",
        "uncertainties": [
          "The candidate joins historically distinct discoveries, so a single, precise prize citation would require careful scope."
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "rank": 5,
      "ballot_id": "B010",
      "proposed_laureates": [
        "Claudio Castelnovo",
        "Roderich Moessner",
        "Steven T. Bramwell"
      ],
      "assessment": {
        "nobel_worthiness": "Spin ice provided a striking realization of fractionalized excitations behaving as magnetic monopoles in a real material, connecting frustrated magnetism to emergent gauge physics.",
        "attribution": "Castelnovo and Moessner developed the monopole interpretation with Bramwell, whose experimental work was central to the field's emergence.",
        "maturity": "The spin-ice phenomenology is well established, although the monopoles are emergent quasiparticles rather than isolated fundamental magnetic charges.",
        "uncertainties": [
          "The extent to which monopole signatures constitute direct evidence of freely propagating quasiparticles remains interpretation-dependent."
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "rank": 6,
      "ballot_id": "B013",
      "proposed_laureates": [
        "Allan H. MacDonald",
        "Pablo Jarillo-Herrero",
        "Rafi Bistritzer"
      ],
      "assessment": {
        "nobel_worthiness": "Magic-angle twisted bilayer graphene revealed tunable flat-band quantum matter and opened a major platform for studying correlated and superconducting phases.",
        "attribution": "Bistritzer and MacDonald predicted the flat bands, while Jarillo-Herrero's group established the landmark experimental correlated phases. The proposed credit captures theory and realization.",
        "maturity": "The platform has generated extensive experimental work, but its phase diagram and the mechanisms of superconductivity remain active research questions.",
        "uncertainties": [
          "The microscopic origin of superconductivity and the degree to which results generalize across moiré materials are unsettled."
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "rank": 7,
      "ballot_id": "B001",
      "proposed_laureates": [
        "Eli Yablonovitch",
        "John D. Joannopoulos",
        "Sajeev John"
      ],
      "assessment": {
        "nobel_worthiness": "Photonic crystals and photonic band gaps showed how periodic dielectric structures can control light propagation and emission, establishing a broad field with enduring applications.",
        "attribution": "Yablonovitch and John independently articulated key ideas, while Joannopoulos made major theoretical contributions to the field. The three names are reasonable for the stated scope.",
        "maturity": "The underlying physics is mature and widely demonstrated, with continuing developments in devices and engineered photonic structures.",
        "uncertainties": [
          "The discovery's scope spans several related contributions, so the relative credit for the original concept, theory, and subsequent development is not singular."
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "rank": 8,
      "ballot_id": "B009",
      "proposed_laureates": [
        "Anthony A. Hyman",
        "Clifford P. Brangwynne",
        "Frank Jülicher"
      ],
      "assessment": {
        "nobel_worthiness": "The identification of biomolecular condensates as liquid-like, phase-separated cellular compartments brought statistical physics into a major area of cell biology and suggested new ways to understand intracellular organization.",
        "attribution": "Hyman and Brangwynne led influential experimental discoveries, while Jülicher contributed to the physical theory. The credit spans discovery and theoretical interpretation.",
        "maturity": "The area has grown rapidly, but mechanisms, functional roles, and the prevalence of liquid-liquid phase separation in cells remain under active scrutiny.",
        "uncertainties": [
          "Some proposed condensates may be better described by other forms of assembly, and the generality of phase separation as an explanation for cellular organization remains contested."
        ]
      }
    }
  ]
